3.0.1
The follow-up release for the defects v3.0.0's own deletions produced, and every one of them is an instrument answering a question next to the one asked. 6d's form-4 grep self-matched: `grep -l <name> .claude/agents/*.md` includes the file under test, which matches its own `name:` frontmatter, so EVERY retired agent reported as still referenced and the deletion the sweep exists to authorise was blocked by the sweep. Note the polarity, because it is the opposite of everything else in the skill: this fails toward REFUSAL, which reads as the safe answer, so a consumer stops and concludes the sweep caught something real. They looked twice only because all three reported referenced — "too tidy; one would have looked plausible" — which is the transferable lesson: UNIFORMITY ACROSS INDEPENDENT SUBJECTS IS EVIDENCE ABOUT THE INSTRUMENT, NOT THE SUBJECTS. Step 2b now prints the absolute resolved backup path: `/tmp` is drive-relative on Windows, so from Python with TMPDIR unset `os.path.abspath('/tmp/…')` yields `G:\tmp\…` and not MSYS `/tmp` — the copy succeeded and only the REPORTED path was wrong, on the one step whose entire job is protecting unrecoverable files, and a user told `/tmp/...` who finds nothing there concludes the backup did not happen. Same class as the `\r`-in-`check-ignore` and the `which`-resolved `bash -n`: a shell idiom means something different one layer over. The RETRO ledger counted budget warnings as errors one line before the budget split, so one consumer's ledger was 30 rows of liveness ceilings and zero real failures — but the fix is emphatically NOT to suppress the row, because rows that vanish from `retro.md` read as "clean" rather than "changed" to anyone grepping an agent id. THE LEDGER IS THE RECORD, THE BRIEF IS THE VIEW: the row stays, `retro-brief.sh` filters, and it filters on neither `errors=` nor `budget=` alone, because the measured row `dead=[Bash,Edit] | budget=0 | errors=2` had a fabricated errors count and an exactly-true dead list and produced two real defect reports — a grant gap is not a failure, the agent completes and silently delivers something weaker. The brief also dedupes by agent id before tailing, after one long-running agent held 11 of 30 rows and hid three of four agents behind a heading promising the last 10 subagent entries. Check 32 is 6d pointed at the repository that ships 6d: three v3.0.0 docs named retired agents as live, found by a reader because 6d scopes to a consumer's project tree and nothing scoped to the toolkit's own `docs/`. It keys on a retirement MARKER rather than the bare name — a bare-name scan flags every migration note and reproduces the 28:1 noise 6d already rejected — keeps the retired set explicit rather than inferred, and fails toward flagging on purpose. The skill's tag-type claim was false and is now a rule: tag type VARIES, always deref with `^{commit}`, because `git tag -a` makes annotated tags while a release cut through the GitHub API makes lightweight ones, which is why v2.3.0 is lightweight. And the five binding forms are now stated as EXHAUSTIVE BY DESIGN: a consumer added a sixth bare-name arm having just read the 28:1 warning and got 9 benign hits, framing it as "I was not being careless, I was being thorough" — thoroughness is exactly what the shape-keyed design defends against.
